    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lithium-ion battery formation affects battery manufacturing time and performance. To design optimized formation protocols, we need a better understanding of solid electrolyte interphase (SEI) formation. In this work, we systematically examine SEI formation on graphite nanoplatelet negative electrodes in two carbonate electrolytes. Graphite nanoplatelets have phase transitions like commercial graphite and larger surface area for better studying the SEI. The standard electrolyte LP40 provides baseline trends, while the electrolyte containing 2 wt.% of the additive vinylene carbonate (VC) represents commercial compositions. We show that the extent of the ethylene carbonate (EC) reduction reaction, occurring at ~0.4-1.0 V during first lithiation, does not impact second-cycle Coulombic efficiency; only the SEI formed at potentials <0.4 V passivates the electrode. Graphite phase transition hysteresis, a proxy for SEI resistance, does not change with time spent either above or below 0.4 V. Thus, it is unlikely resistance is driving Coulombic efficiency or capacity differences. The VC-containing electrolyte exhibits similar electrochemical trends. Therefore, cycling quickly through the EC reduction voltage region can decrease formation time while maintaining battery performance. We also show, using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, that SEI chemical composition in the LP40 electrolyte is not linked to formation protocol, suggesting a microstructural factor for battery performance. This work reveals fundamental processes of SEI growth on graphitic negative electrodes and provides insights for optimizing battery formation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Davidoff, I. (2024). Effects of Formation Cycling and Electrolyte Additive on Solid Electrolyte Interphase Passivation, Composition, and Kinetics. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/nv122jt5083. https://doi.org/10.25740/nv122jt5083.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
